{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788369191,"nonce":"ehuvhYCpZyYqMhIbPqk8lSkP9Pw=","message":"Implemented on `fix/e440676-error-class`, commit `f79bae1`. **Not run against a bench** — builds clean (`dotnet build -c Release`, 0/0), no assembly on the branch, and `accept/4.2-play-loop.py --selftest` passes with every mutation still caught.\n\n**Counts re-derived before building anything**, from `transcripts/m1-20260901*/*/result.json`: 691 `ok:false`, 325 `bad-args` / 200 `busy` / 164 `unread-journal` / 2 `rwa-game-down`. The issue's figures are exact.\n\n**§1, §2 — the vocabulary.** `error.class`, four values. `refused` (the bench said no and the answer is actionable — your next call must DIFFER) · `flow` (nothing is wrong, ask again — the SAME call is correct later) · `fault` (something broke in here) · `client` (`rwa-…`, the mod never saw the command). The three classifications that took an argument:\n\n- `stale-on-restart` is **flow**: the command predated the session and was answered rather than replayed, so the caller resends it UNCHANGED if it still wants it — which is the flow test exactly.\n- `cannot-set-speed` (`TimeDriver`, not in the issue's list) is **flow** on the strength of its own detail string, which already said *\"Nothing was armed; retry when the game hands control back\"*.\n- `bad-json` is **refused** even though nothing parsed, because the fix is on the caller's side and is not \"wait\".\n\n**§3 — derived, and the shape matters.** Both candidate designs were written before either was committed. A `switch (code)` at the serializer IS the table the issue rules out: a new code falls through its default, silently, and the default reads as an answer. So the class is carried BY the code — `Result.Fail` takes a `Runtime.ErrCode` (a readonly struct of `(Code, Class)`) and **there is no string overload**, so the compiler asks the question at every site that could invent a code. `Poller.BuildResultJson` then derives it in one line, `r.ErrorCode.Class`, with no branch to get wrong. Eleven codes, eleven declarations: `grep -rn 'ErrCode\\.\\(Refused\\|Flow\\|Fault\\)(' Source/AutoRimmer/`. `rwa` stamps one constant, `E_CLASS = \"client\"`, since every `rwa-` code is client-side by construction.\n\n**§4 — the verdict changed, the detection did not.** `accept/4.2-play-loop.py` FAILed on every `unread-journal` refusal. Measured across the chain: **159 of the 164 were ANSWERED** — a `journal` call, or a declared `unread_ok`, before the loop asked for time again — and **5 were not**. The 5 are `m1-20260831`'s exact shape and still FAIL; the 159 are now an `INFO` line naming the guard, plus an `INFO` for any `flow` refusal an advance collected. The forward scan is deliberately not \"the next command\": the loop's turn is `things → build → journal → advance`, and 86 of the 164 were followed by a `digest` that went on to journal. Verified by running the auditor over the real chain — `159 … OBEYED` and a FAIL naming `m1-20260901/127-advance`, `-s00/329`, `-s02/374`, `-s02/642`, `-s03/047`.\n\n**§5 — the cockpit.** Red is spent on `fault` only; the class word leads the line (`flow · busy`, `refused · unread-journal`). No new palette tier — one warning colour is that file's own rule, and `1202796` is untouched. An envelope from before this shipped has no class, keeps today's red, and reads as `error · \u003ccode\u003e` exactly as it did.\n\n**Acceptance**: `accept/e440676-error-class.md` — ten `rwa` calls with one expectation each, plus the two source-level invariants no bench call can show because they are about what cannot be written.\n\n**Not demonstrated**: nothing was dispatched through a live poller. No `error.class` has ever been written to a result file.","files":null}]}